**The missing case is a zero-lambda operand.** This valid reader has j at depth 1:

```text
R84 = \s. (\x. s (\b t. b (x t) (c t x)))
          (s (\b t. b (\a k. k a) (t t)) j)
```

Its immediate j-parent is

```text
X = s (\b t. b (\a k. k a) (t t)) j
```

and satisfies

```text
X[Z(r)]   →* \k. k j
X[U_n(N)] →* N                 for every n ≥1.
```

The wrapper applies X to r on zero and returns `c U_(n−1) X` on unary. Consequently, R84 satisfies both reader laws. `rd.check(..., nmax=8, verbose=False)` returned:

```text
(True, (84, 80, 1, 8, 14, 12, 1, 1))
```

Thus touching X does **not** force its zero normal form to be `r j` or `j`. Its unary value is rigid, so the unary-lambda exception does not capture this mechanism. It lies outside the four listed forms. Its weight is 84: this refutes the structural argument, **not** the theorem’s ≤45 implication. A quantitative exclusion of this missing case is still needed.

**§11.2 also has a counterexample satisfying §11.1’s actual hypothesis:**

```text
R104 = \s. (\u. (\x. s (\b t. b (x s) x))
                  (s (\b t. b (\a q. t u) (c t)) (s s s))) j
```

Choose the source unary-output anchor

```text
X2 = s (\b t. b (\a q. t u) (c t)) (s s s).
```

Only u binds above X2 within Y. Its occurrence is erased by the first unary F, hence unary-dispensable for every n. Nevertheless,

```text
X2[Z, u:=j]   →* \q. r j
X2[U_n]       →* c U_(n−1) N.
```

Replacing X2 by rigid h makes the zero wrapper return `h Z(r)`, outside the enumerated `{r j, h j, h, r h}`. The reader passed `rd.check` through n=8 at weight 104; the displayed reductions establish the all-n behavior.

**There is a useful finite repair for §11.** I checked the unary wrapper law without imposing any zero-output filter. Among all **6,089** charged-cost ≤18 terms, there were **53** unary-law survivors and **zero caps**. Exactly one survivor was omitted by the original filter:

```text
charged cost 17: \u. s I x       zero result: r
```

Therefore **no survivor is omitted through charged cost 16**, covering the proposed exclusion and equality budgets. This repairs the filter at the relevant budget without asserting the false general persistence lemma.

**The index accounting is sound.** With k enclosing binders, an original s/c leaf saves k bits. Replacing an occurrence of the ith outer-to-inner binder by s saves `k−i ≥0`; internal binder depth cancels. Joint dispensability ensures those replacement leaves remain dispensable. Thus

```text
|X2| − |T|
  = k · (# original s/c leaves)
    + Σ_i (k−i) · (# replaced xi leaves)
  ≥ k · F_need(T).
```

The asserted XB bounds follow from the two small arms and the necessary s/c leaves.

**The remaining theorem-level problem is minimality.** Removing a transparent wrapper produces a different, lighter reader, potentially outside R1. That contradicts **global minimality**, but does not establish a lower bound for every reader in the original subclass. The same unstated assumption appears in 10.1 when making C′ trivial and discarding shape B/E.

A defensible repaired §11 target is: **a globally minimum reader with the specified source anchor and dispensable-binder hypothesis cannot have w≤45**. The statement about every R1 reader, and its universal equality claim, needs further justification.

Verification reproduced the P-lemma’s 9,265-term check, the 53,537-term arm check, and the small wrapper checks. All 17 capped original arm evaluations were independently rejected by a supplementary Python non-equality checker; this is not Lean certification. I inspected the larger generators and stored logs without rerunning their enumerations. The combined 25-bit log omits individual operand caps, so I do not independently certify that entire cap audit.

**Recommendation:** retain the dispensability and index-shift results; downgrade the four-family exhaustion; replace §11’s zero-shape premise with the unary-only finite check; state global minimality explicitly.

